## 8. Known Limitations

These come from deliberate adversarial testing (Phase VI) and are documented honestly, with the mitigations attempted.

### 8.1 Jailbreak resistance is only as strong as the prompt
With only a basic scope instruction, the model told jokes and answered general questions when asked to ignore its instructions. Adding an explicit anti-override section and few-shot examples made it refuse the **exact phrasings** shown in the examples, but a **close paraphrase** still got through, which shows the 1.5B model matches specific phrasing rather than generalising the rule.

A deterministic regex pre-filter was built and worked on the paraphrases tested, but it was **removed** because the assignment requires that every response come from prompt orchestration and conversational memory alone. The final defence is rule 7, placed last in the system prompt where small models weight instructions most (an effect observed during testing). Expect it to be imperfect: some phrasings of an override request will still succeed. This is the main accepted limitation of a prompt-only design at this model size.

### 8.2 Catalogue errors and hallucination
Even after compacting the catalogue, placing it near the end of the prompt, and adding explicit "do not invent titles" rules with a named example, the model sometimes:
- invents titles that are not in the catalogue (for example fake Harry Potter sequels),
- contradicts the available-copies number (for example calling The Hobbit unavailable when 1 copy is available),
- mislabels genres or omits matching books when asked to list a whole category.

Exact lookup across about 140 structured values (28 books x 5 fields) is much harder for a 1.5B model than fluent text generation. Even single-title questions failed in later testing: with 1 of 4 copies on the shelf, The Hobbit was reported as checked out in several chats, mostly when the patron used the word "buy", which the first prompt never addressed. Wrong answers then stayed in the 12-message window and were repeated. The model also answered questions about other patrons' holds, for which it has no data. Mitigations added after that test: each catalogue line now carries an explicit AVAILABLE or CHECKED OUT status instead of two bare numbers, and the prompt now states that the library lends rather than sells and that the assistant cannot see other patrons' accounts or holds.

### 8.3 Repetition anchoring
Early on, the model sometimes repeated its previous refusal verbatim whatever the new question was, because the exact refusal sentence appeared several times in the context. Mitigated by removing duplicate examples so the sentence appears once in the prompt, and by rule 4 ("each new message is a fresh question").

### 8.4 Long term memory limitations
- The notes are written by a 1.5B model and can be incomplete or wrong. In one test they recorded a hold on a book the patron never mentioned and inferred a favourite author from a single question, because the note taker was reading the assistant's own (sometimes wrong) replies. The note taker now reads only the patron's messages and is told not to infer. The patron can see and clear the notes in the sidebar.
- Notes are only updated when a chat ends. In one test a chat that was closed unexpectedly never produced notes, so the disconnect handler now always ends the session in a `finally` block, including when the connection drops mid-reply. Killing the server process or losing power still loses the last chat's notes.
- After a chat ends, the first message of the next chat can be delayed while the note update finishes and the prompt is reprocessed.
- One notes file serves all users of the demo, because there is no login.

